MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m16",
  "summary": "Explain how power reaches the wheels through the clutch, manual gearbox and driveshafts, and diagnose and service these parts safely.",
  "outcomes": ["Describe front-, rear- and all-wheel-drive layouts and calculate a gear ratio and final drive.","Name the parts of a friction clutch, explain how it is operated and recognise slipping and dragging.","Explain how a manual gearbox with constant-mesh gears and synchronisers transmits power in each gear.","Describe propeller shafts, universal joints and constant-velocity joints and their typical faults.","Diagnose hard shifting, jumping out of gear, noise, leaks and clutch judder.","Change gearbox oil, replace a clutch in the correct order and bleed a hydraulic clutch."],
  "cheatsheet": ["Clutch parts: flywheel, clutch disc, pressure plate, release bearing, and the operating system.","Slipping clutch: engine speed rises but the vehicle does not speed up. Dragging clutch: hard to select gears when stopped.","Gear ratio = driven teeth ÷ driving teeth. A low gear has a high ratio and gives more torque.","Synchronisers match the speed of the gear and shaft so you can change gear without grinding.","A clicking noise on full-lock turns usually means a worn outer CV joint, often from a split boot.","Vibration under acceleration often points to a worn inner CV joint or propeller shaft joint.","Check the fill plug opens before you drain gearbox oil, so you do not trap yourself with no way to refill.","When replacing a clutch, align the disc with a tool, replace the release bearing and tighten the pressure plate in a cross pattern.","Support the gearbox with a transmission jack. It is heavy."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m16-l1", "title": "Driveline layout", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The driveline carries power from the engine to the wheels. This lesson explains the common layouts and how the gear ratios combine."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Drive layouts"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Front-wheel drive (FWD):** the engine and gearbox (together called the transaxle) are at the front and drive the front wheels through driveshafts. It is compact and gives good traction in many conditions.","**Rear-wheel drive (RWD):** the engine is at the front and drives the rear wheels through a clutch, gearbox, propeller shaft and a rear differential. It is common on trucks and larger cars.","**All-wheel or four-wheel drive (AWD, 4WD):** power goes to all four wheels. Module 17 covers it."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The torque path"},
      {"t":"p","text":"On a rear-wheel-drive vehicle, the path is: engine → flywheel → clutch → gearbox → propeller shaft → differential → axle shafts → wheels. Each stage can multiply torque or change speed. On a front-wheel-drive vehicle with a transaxle, the gearbox, the final drive and the differential are in one housing, and short driveshafts with constant-velocity joints go to the wheels."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Gear ratios"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **gear ratio** shows how many times the input turns for one turn of the output. It is the number of teeth on the driven gear divided by the number on the driving gear. A **low gear**, such as first, has a high ratio (for example 3.5:1) which multiplies the torque and gives strong acceleration but low speed. A **high gear**, such as fifth, has a low ratio (for example 0.8:1) and gives a lower engine speed at cruising speed."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Final drive"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **final drive** is the last reduction, in the differential, with a ratio of about 3:1 to 5:1. The overall ratio in any gear is the gearbox ratio multiplied by the final drive ratio."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Overall ratio","steps":["Second gear is 1.9:1 and the final drive is 4.1:1.","Overall ratio = 1.9 × 4.1 = 7.79:1.","At an engine speed of 3,000 rpm, the wheels turn at 3,000 ÷ 7.79 = about 385 rpm."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Drive layouts compared"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Layout","Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["Front engine, front drive (FWD)","Compact, light, good traction in rain on the driven wheels, low cost","Heavy front end, torque steer under power, wear of front tyres"],["Front engine, rear drive (RWD)","Good balance, strong traction when loaded, easy to tow","More parts and weight, tunnel intrudes into the cabin"],["Rear engine, rear drive","Excellent traction from the rear weight","Tail-heavy handling, cooling and luggage space problems"],["All-wheel drive (AWD)","Best traction in poor conditions","Heavier, more complex, higher cost and fuel use"],["Mid engine","Best balance for sports cars","Space and cooling problems"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"The layout decides what a technician will find under the car. A transaxle with two driveshafts is typical of FWD, and a gearbox with a long propeller shaft to a rear axle is typical of RWD. Learn the layout of the vehicle before you start work so that you know which parts to inspect."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Torque is multiplied and speed is reduced by the same ratio. A low gear trades speed for pulling power."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Check the drive layout in the manual before you lift a vehicle or remove parts. It tells you which parts you will meet and where."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m16-l2", "title": "Gear ratios, speed and torque calculations", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Every gear change trades speed for force. A gearbox lets a small engine move a heavy vehicle from a standstill, and then lets it cruise at low engine speed. This lesson shows how to work with gear ratios, calculate road speed and torque at the wheels, and understand why the gears are chosen as they are."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Ratios in each gear"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Gear","Gearbox ratio","Final drive","Overall ratio"],"rows":[["1st","3.58","4.10","14.68"],["2nd","2.04","4.10","8.36"],["3rd","1.32","4.10","5.41"],["4th","0.98","4.10","4.02"],["5th","0.78","4.10","3.20"],["Reverse","3.40","4.10","13.94"]],"caption":"Overall ratio = gearbox ratio × final drive ratio. The values are typical for a small car."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Road speed (km/h) = engine rpm × tyre circumference (m) × 60 ÷ (overall ratio × 1,000)"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Speed in 3rd and 5th gear at 3,000 rpm","steps":["The tyre has a rolling circumference of 1.985 m.","In 3rd the overall ratio is 5.41. Wheel speed = 3,000 ÷ 5.41 = 554 rpm. Speed = 554 × 1.985 × 60 ÷ 1,000 = 66.0 km/h.","In 5th the overall ratio is 3.20. Wheel speed = 3,000 ÷ 3.20 = 938 rpm. Speed = 938 × 1.985 × 60 ÷ 1,000 = 111.7 km/h.","At 111.7 km/h in 5th, the engine turns at 3,000 rpm. A higher top gear, with a ratio of 0.65, would lower this to about 2,500 rpm, saving fuel and noise but reducing pulling power."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Torque at the wheels"},
      {"t":"example","title":"How much force pushes the car in first gear?","steps":["The engine gives 200 N·m. In 1st, the overall ratio is 14.68. If the drivetrain is 92% efficient, the torque at the wheels is 200 × 14.68 × 0.92 = 2,701 N·m.","The tyre's rolling radius is 0.316 m. The force at the road is 2,701 ÷ 0.316 = 8,548 N.","On a car of 1,400 kg, the force gives an acceleration of 8,548 ÷ 1,400 = 6.1 m/s², if there were no drag or resistance and the tyres did not slip. In practice about half this is available, but it shows why first gear gives strong acceleration.","The same engine torque in 5th gives 200 × 3.20 × 0.92 = 589 N·m at the wheels, just over a fifth. This is why you change down to climb a hill."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"How gears are chosen"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**First gear** must be low enough to move the vehicle from rest on a steep hill with a full load, without too much clutch slip.","**Top gear** is chosen so that the engine turns at an economical speed at cruising speed, and not too slowly, which would cause labouring.","The **steps** between gears are chosen so that the engine speed falls into its power band after each change. Ratios often form a geometric series, each gear about 1.3 to 1.6 times the next.","A **final drive** ratio is chosen to give the overall performance. Changing it affects every gear."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Changing tyre size"},
      {"t":"example","title":"The effect of larger tyres","steps":["A car originally had a tyre with a circumference of 1.985 m. A larger tyre of 2.10 m is fitted.","At the same engine speed, the car goes 2.10 ÷ 1.985 = 1.058 times as fast, about 5.8% more. The speedometer, which counts rotations, now reads 5.8% low: at a real 100 km/h it shows only about 94.5 km/h.","The engine has to push a larger tyre, so acceleration is a little lower. In effect, a taller tyre acts like a taller final drive ratio."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A low gear ratio multiplies force and cuts speed. A high gear ratio does the reverse. The product of the ratios gives the overall effect."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Keep a table of ratios for the vehicles you often work on. It makes noise diagnosis and speed checks much quicker."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m16-l3", "title": "The clutch", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"In a manual vehicle, the clutch connects the engine to the gearbox so the vehicle can move off smoothly and change gear. This lesson explains how it works."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"clutch-assembly"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Main parts"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Flywheel:** bolted to the crankshaft. Its flat face is one of the friction surfaces.","**Clutch disc (friction plate):** has friction material on both sides and is splined to the gearbox input shaft. Springs in its hub soften the engagement.","**Pressure plate:** bolted to the flywheel, it clamps the disc against the flywheel. A **diaphragm spring** supplies the clamping force.","**Release (throw-out) bearing:** presses on the centre of the diaphragm spring to release the clamp when the pedal is pressed.","**Pilot bearing or bush:** supports the end of the input shaft in the crankshaft or flywheel."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"How it works"},
      {"t":"p","text":"With the pedal up, the pressure plate clamps the disc between itself and the flywheel, and power flows to the gearbox. When you press the pedal, the release bearing pushes on the diaphragm spring, which pulls the pressure plate back, and the disc is free. The engine can then turn without turning the gearbox. When you release the pedal, the disc is clamped again, and the friction between the surfaces takes up the drive smoothly."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"clutch-operation"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Operating systems"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **hydraulic** clutch uses a master cylinder on the pedal and a slave cylinder at the gearbox, joined by a fluid pipe. It needs fluid, usually brake fluid, and bleeding if air gets in. A **cable** clutch uses a steel cable from the pedal to a release lever, and may need adjustment for free play. **Free play** is the small movement of the pedal before the release bearing begins to act. Too little free play can cause slipping, and too much can stop the clutch releasing completely."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Dual-mass flywheel"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many modern engines use a **dual-mass flywheel (DMF)**, which has two parts joined by springs, to absorb the engine's vibrations. It can wear, causing a rattle at idle or when the engine stops, and judder. It is costly, and is usually replaced with the clutch."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Slipping: engine revs rise but the speed does not","Worn disc, oil on the disc, weak pressure plate, no free play"],["Dragging: hard to select gears, grinding, creeping when stopped","Air in hydraulics, too much free play, warped disc, worn release system"],["Judder when moving off","Oil on the disc, hot spots on the flywheel, worn engine or gearbox mounts"],["Noise when the pedal is pressed","Worn release bearing or pilot bearing"]],"caption":"Clutch symptoms"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Clutch wear and service life"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The friction lining on a clutch disc wears every time the clutch slips, as in a hill start or a slow manoeuvre. A typical life is 100,000 to 200,000 km, but it can be less than 30,000 km with a heavy load or rough use. A **self-adjusting clutch** moves the pressure plate fingers as the disc wears, so the pedal height and feel stay the same until the disc is worn out. A wear indicator may show the remaining life. A worn disc shows as a rising biting point, a pedal that is higher and a slipping clutch under load."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Sign","What it indicates"],"rows":[["Engine speed rises with no matching increase in road speed under load","A slipping clutch, worn or contaminated lining, or a weak pressure plate"],["Biting point very high on the pedal travel","A worn disc or a stretched cable"],["Judder when pulling away","Oil on the lining, hot spots on the flywheel, a warped disc or loose mounts"],["Pedal stays on the floor, or it is hard to get into gear","Hydraulic or cable fault, or a broken diaphragm finger"],["Grinding noise when the pedal is down","A worn release bearing, which is noisy when the pedal is pressed"],["Rattle at idle that stops when the pedal is pressed","A worn input shaft bearing or a worn dual-mass flywheel"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Clutch friction material of older vehicles may contain asbestos. Do not blow dust with compressed air. Use a approved cleaner and follow the dust procedure for brake and clutch dust."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Do not ride the clutch pedal or hold the vehicle on a hill with it. Both wear the disc quickly. Tell the customer."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m16-l4", "title": "Clutch design, torque capacity and hydraulics", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A clutch has to carry the engine's full torque without slipping, and yet engage smoothly. This lesson explains how its capacity is worked out, how the hydraulic or cable control multiplies the driver's effort and what the main faults mean. The numbers show why a clutch that is fine in an ordinary car can fail under a heavy load."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How much torque can it carry?"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Clutch torque capacity = μ × clamp force × mean radius × number of friction surfaces","caption":"μ is the friction coefficient of the lining, typically 0.25 to 0.4"},
      {"t":"example","title":"A typical car clutch","steps":["The diaphragm spring clamps the disc with a force of 6,000 N. The lining's friction coefficient is 0.30. The mean radius of the friction area is 0.10 m. A single disc has two friction faces.","Torque capacity = 0.30 × 6,000 × 0.10 × 2 = 360 N·m.","This must be higher than the engine's peak torque, with a margin of 20 to 50 per cent. If the engine makes 250 N·m, the clutch holds it. If someone tunes the engine to 380 N·m, the clutch slips and wears quickly. A heavier-duty pressure plate, a larger disc or twin discs are then fitted.","Oil on the lining halves μ to 0.15 or less, so the capacity falls to 180 N·m and the clutch slips even at modest power."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Slipping and heat"},
      {"t":"p","text":"While the clutch slips during engagement, the speed difference times the torque is turned into heat. This is why hill starts with a heavy load, towing and long periods of slow-speed driving overheat a clutch. At high temperature, the friction material fades, glazes and burns, and the pressure plate and flywheel may crack or show blue heat marks. A burnt smell after a hill start shows that the clutch has overheated."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The diaphragm spring"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A diaphragm spring is a dished steel disc with fingers. When the clutch is engaged, the spring is almost flat and pushes on the pressure plate. When the release bearing pushes the fingers, the spring flips and pulls the plate away. As the lining wears, the spring flattens a little more and the clamping force changes slightly. A weak or broken spring reduces the capacity and makes the pedal feel odd."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The hydraulic control"},
      {"t":"example","title":"How the pedal effort is multiplied","steps":["The pedal has a lever ratio of 4:1, so a foot force of 100 N gives 400 N on the master cylinder piston.","The master cylinder piston is 19 mm in diameter, with an area of 283.5 mm². Pressure = 400 ÷ 283.5 = 1.41 N/mm², or 14.1 bar.","The slave cylinder piston is 25.4 mm in diameter, with an area of 506.7 mm². The force at the slave cylinder = 1.41 × 506.7 = 715 N.","Travel works the other way. If the pedal moves 150 mm, the master piston moves 150 ÷ 4 = 37.5 mm. The slave piston moves 37.5 × (283.5 ÷ 506.7) = 21 mm. Only part of this moves the release bearing, since some travel is lost in the system."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Air in the hydraulic system compresses and takes up the travel, giving a soft pedal that does not release the clutch fully. A leak in the master or slave cylinder lets the pedal sink, and fluid loss may also show as a falling fluid level in the brake reservoir, which usually supplies the clutch. Many cars have a **concentric slave cylinder** around the input shaft, inside the bell housing, that pushes the release bearing directly. A leak can only be repaired by removing the gearbox."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cable clutches"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["A cable clutch has a steel cable inside a sheath. Friction in the cable gives a heavier pedal as it ages, and it may snap, with no warning.","Check the cable's routing, the sheath ends and the adjuster. A stretched cable shows as a rising biting point.","Many cable clutches have a **self-adjuster**. Do not pull the pedal up to adjust it unless the manual says so."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Dual-mass flywheel in more depth"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A dual-mass flywheel has a primary mass on the crankshaft and a secondary mass that carries the clutch, joined by springs and a damper. It absorbs the torsional vibration of the engine, especially the lumpy torque of a diesel, so the gearbox runs quietly. The springs wear and the grease dries out. Symptoms are a rattle at idle that vanishes when the clutch pedal is pressed, vibration at low speed, and a shudder when pulling away. Check the **play** of the secondary mass against the primary mass, as the manual states: free movement beyond the limit means it is worn. A worn DMF should be replaced at the same time as the clutch, because the labour is shared."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A clutch that is too weak for the engine slips, and one that is contaminated slips even sooner. Find the cause of any slipping before you fit a new disc."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never use compressed air to blow clutch dust. It may contain fine particles that are harmful to breathe. Use a wet method or a vacuum with a suitable filter."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m16-l5", "title": "The manual gearbox", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The manual gearbox offers a set of gear ratios and a reverse. This lesson explains how it is built and how power passes through it in each gear."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"manual-gearbox-power-flow"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Shafts and gears"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Input shaft:** receives power from the clutch disc. Its gear is always in mesh with the layshaft.","**Layshaft (countershaft):** a shaft with a cluster of fixed gears that always turns when the input shaft turns.","**Output shaft (main shaft):** carries the gears that turn freely on it. The selected gear is locked to the shaft to carry power out.","**Reverse idler gear:** an extra gear that reverses the direction of rotation."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Constant mesh and synchronisers"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a **constant-mesh** gearbox, all the gears are always engaged with each other. The gears on the output shaft spin freely until one is locked to the shaft. The locking is done by a **synchroniser** (synchromesh) unit. When you move the shift lever, a **selector fork** slides a sleeve towards the gear. A brass or steel cone first presses against the gear and matches their speeds by friction, and then the sleeve slides over the gear's teeth, locking it to the shaft. Synchronisers prevent grinding."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Power flow"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a gear, power flows from the input shaft to the layshaft through the constant-mesh gear pair, along the layshaft, and then through the chosen gear pair to the output shaft. In **fourth gear** on many gearboxes, called direct drive, the input and output shafts are locked together and the layshaft idles. In neutral, no gear is locked to the output shaft."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Gearbox oil"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The gearbox oil lubricates the gears and the synchronisers, carries away heat and protects against wear. Types differ. Many use a gear oil of a grade such as 75W-90, and some use automatic transmission fluid or a special manual transmission fluid. The wrong oil can make shifting hard, or damage soft metal parts such as brass synchronisers. Always use the specification in the manual and check the level at the fill plug."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Noises"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Whine** in one gear: a worn gear or bearing.","**Rumble** at all times, changing with speed: worn bearings.","**Clunk** when you change from drive to coast: worn driveline parts or backlash.","**Grinding** when you shift: worn synchronisers or a dragging clutch."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Gear selection mechanism"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A shift lever or cable moves a **selector shaft** that engages the correct **selector fork** with the sleeve for a pair of gears. An **interlock** stops two gears from being engaged at once, which would lock the gearbox. A **detent ball and spring** holds each fork in position, and a **reverse lock-out** stops reverse being selected by mistake. Wear in the selector parts gives a vague gear lever with too much play, difficulty finding gears or a gear that jumps out. The cable bushes and the lever's ball joint are common wear points. Adjust the linkage only as the manual says."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The oil level is correct when oil is level with the bottom of the fill hole, with the vehicle level. Too low causes wear, and too high causes leaks and foaming."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If a gearbox is noisy in only one gear, the fault is in that gear pair. If it is noisy in all gears, suspect a bearing or the oil."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m16-l6", "title": "Automated manual and dual-clutch transmissions", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Many modern cars have a gearbox that looks like a manual gearbox inside, but changes gear by itself. Two main types are the automated manual transmission (AMT) and the dual-clutch transmission (DCT). They offer the efficiency of a manual with the convenience of an automatic. They also behave differently from both, and they fail in ways that a technician must be able to recognise."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Automated manual transmission (AMT)"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An AMT is a manual gearbox with a single clutch. Electric or hydraulic **actuators**, controlled by a computer, press the clutch and move the selector forks. It is cheap, light and efficient. The gear changes are slow, as the clutch must be released and the next gear engaged, so the drive is interrupted and the car can lurch (a \"head nod\"). The clutch is worn by the automatic engagement, and a worn clutch or a worn actuator gives rough changes. After the clutch is replaced, the computer must **relearn the clutch touch point** with a scan tool."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Dual-clutch transmission (DCT)"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A DCT has two clutches. One drives the odd gears (1, 3, 5) through a solid shaft, and the other drives the even gears (2, 4, 6) through a hollow shaft around the first. While the car is running in 3rd gear, the computer selects 4th on the other shaft, ready. At the moment of the gear change, one clutch releases as the other engages, with no break in the drive. The change takes a fraction of a second."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","Clutch design","Features"],"rows":[["Dry DCT","Two dry friction clutches, like a manual's","Efficient and light. Used with lower torque engines. Can overheat in heavy traffic, with judder at low speed."],["Wet DCT","Two multi-plate clutches running in oil","Handles more torque, smoother, more cooling. Needs the right oil, and a regular oil and filter change."]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The mechatronic unit"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A DCT is controlled by a **mechatronic unit**, a block that has solenoid valves, sensors and a control module, often attached to the gearbox. It moves the clutches and the selectors. Faults in the sensors, the solenoids, the pressure or the software give gear changes that are harsh, slow or missing, a warning light, and a limp mode that allows only some gears. The unit may be repaired or replaced, and the new one must be **coded and adapted** to the gearbox."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Typical faults"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Possible cause"],"rows":[["Judder or shudder pulling away","Worn clutch, a contaminated clutch, a failing mechatronic unit, or a software adaptation that has drifted"],["Hesitation and jerky low-speed driving","Dry clutch heat, a worn clutch, or an engine fault such as a misfire"],["Harsh gear changes","Low oil pressure, a worn solenoid, or a bad adaptation"],["Gearbox warning light and limp mode","A sensor or a solenoid fault, a low fluid level, an electrical supply problem"],["Noise from the gearbox","Bearings, a worn clutch release bearing, or a rattling dual-mass flywheel"],["Leak of oil at the bell housing","Input shaft seals"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"What a technician can do"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Check for **software updates**. Many DCT faults are fixed by an update.","Use the scan tool to read fault codes, data and adaptation values, such as clutch touch points and wear.","Check the **battery** and **earth** connections first. Low voltage causes many transmission faults.","Change the oil and the filter at the interval for a wet DCT, with the exact fluid specified, and fill it to the exact level with a scan tool temperature reading.","After a clutch or mechatronic unit replacement, run the **adaptation** with the scan tool. Without it, the gearbox shifts roughly and can wear out quickly.","Dismantling a DCT is a job for a specialist, as is a reprogramming job."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Driving habits that save the clutch"},
      {"t":"p","text":"On a dry DCT or an AMT, the clutch is slipping when the car creeps forward. In traffic, do not use the throttle to hold the car on a slope. Use the brake. On a hill, do not hold the car with the throttle, as the clutch is slipping hard. Tell the customer this at a service, as it saves a costly repair."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"These gearboxes depend on software and fluid as much as on gears and clutches. Check the updates and the oil before replacing hardware."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If a customer complains of judder in slow traffic, ask whether the car has had the latest gearbox software, and when the fluid was last changed."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m16-l7", "title": "Shafts and joints", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Shafts and joints carry the power from the gearbox to the wheels, and must handle the movement of the suspension and steering. This lesson covers the main types and faults."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Propeller shafts and universal joints"},
      {"t":"p","text":"On a rear-wheel-drive vehicle, a **propeller shaft** (driveshaft or prop shaft) joins the gearbox to the differential. It has **universal joints (U-joints)** at each end, that allow the angle to change as the rear axle moves. A long shaft may be in two parts, with a **centre bearing** to support it. A worn U-joint causes a clunk when taking up the drive and a vibration at speed. The shaft must be balanced, and the joints need to be at the correct angles."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Constant-velocity joints"},
      {"t":"p","text":"On front-wheel-drive vehicles, driveshafts have **constant-velocity (CV) joints** at each end. The outer joint at the wheel can work at a sharp angle while the wheel is steering. The inner joint at the gearbox allows the shaft to change length as the suspension moves. A rubber **CV boot** keeps grease in and dirt out. If the boot splits, grease escapes, and dirt and water ruin the joint."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Clicking or popping noise on turns","Worn outer CV joint, usually after a split boot"],["Vibration when accelerating","Worn inner CV joint or damaged shaft"],["Clunk when taking up the drive","Worn U-joint, loose mounts, worn differential parts"],["Vibration at certain speeds","Unbalanced or bent propeller shaft, worn centre bearing"],["Grease thrown inside the wheel arch","Split CV boot"]],"caption":"Shaft and joint faults"},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"driveshaft-joints"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Wheel bearings"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The wheel bearing carries the vehicle's weight and lets the wheel turn. A worn bearing makes a humming or growling noise that gets louder with speed and may change when you steer. Play can be felt by rocking the wheel. Replace the bearing in line with the manual, because a failure can cause the wheel to come off."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Inspection"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Check the CV boots for splits, cracks and grease leaks, and the clamps for tightness.","Rock the shaft by hand to check for play in the joints.","Look at the shaft for dents and the centre bearing rubber for splitting."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"How much does a universal joint change speed?","steps":["A single universal joint turns its output at a speed that rises and falls twice per turn. At a working angle of 10°, the output varies between cos 10° = 0.985 and 1 ÷ cos 10° = 1.015 times the input speed.","This is a change of about ±1.5%. At 3,000 rpm, the output shaft speeds up and slows down by about 45 rpm twice per turn.","Two joints on the same shaft cancel this out if their angles are equal and their yokes are in line (in phase). That is why propeller shafts must be refitted in the same phase, with the marks aligned, and why the joint angles must be correct."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"A split CV boot found early can often be fixed with a new boot and grease. If the joint has been running dry or dirty, replace the whole shaft or joint."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Support the vehicle on stands before you remove or inspect driveshafts, and never go under a vehicle held on a jack alone."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m16-l8", "title": "Driveline noise and vibration diagnosis", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Customers complain of vibration and noise more often than almost anything else, and the causes can lie in tyres, wheels, shafts, joints, gearboxes and engines. A method that links the symptom to a rotating part saves a lot of time. This lesson shows how to use speed, load and frequency to find the part, with worked examples."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Questions the symptom answers"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Question","If yes","If no"],"rows":[["Does it change with vehicle speed?","Tyre, wheel, shaft, bearing or final drive","Engine or accessory"],["Does it change with engine speed in neutral?","Engine, clutch, flywheel, accessory drive","Driveline or wheels"],["Is it worse under acceleration (drive)?","Driveshaft, inner CV joint, mount, differential","Wheel, tyre or bearing"],["Is it worse when coasting?","Gear mesh or a differential problem","Joint or mount problem"],["Does it change when you turn?","Outer CV joint, wheel bearing, tyre","Inner joint, propeller shaft, tyre"],["Does it appear in a narrow speed range?","Out-of-balance or bent rotating part, resonance","Wear or play"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Frequency and rotating parts"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Every rotating part makes a vibration at the frequency of its rotation, called the **first order**. If you know the frequency, you can find which part is turning at that speed. A tyre and wheel turn at wheel speed. A propeller shaft or driveshaft turns at wheel speed times the final drive ratio. An engine turns at engine speed."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Which part vibrates at 14 Hz?","steps":["A car with a tyre circumference of 1.985 m is travelling at 100 km/h. The wheel turns at 27.8 ÷ 1.985 = 14.0 revolutions per second, or 14.0 Hz.","A vibration at 14 Hz at this speed comes from a wheel or tyre: out of balance, a bent wheel, or a tyre with a flat spot or a bulge.","The propeller shaft, with a final drive ratio of 4.1, turns at 14.0 × 4.1 = 57.4 Hz (3,444 rpm). A vibration at 57 Hz at 100 km/h points to the propeller shaft, a universal joint or the pinion.","If the vibration is the same in any gear at the same speed, it comes from the road wheels and driveline. If it changes with the gear at the same road speed, it is related to engine or gearbox speed."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Rotates at","At 100 km/h (example)"],"rows":[["Road wheel and tyre","Wheel speed","About 14 Hz"],["Driveshaft (FWD)","Wheel speed","About 14 Hz"],["Propeller shaft (RWD)","Wheel speed × final drive","About 57 Hz"],["Engine in 5th gear","Wheel speed × overall ratio","About 45 Hz, 2,700 rpm"],["Engine in 3rd gear","Wheel speed × overall ratio","About 76 Hz, 4,540 rpm"]],"caption":"Assuming a wheel speed of 14 Hz, a final drive of 4.1, and overall ratios of 3.20 in 5th and 5.41 in 3rd."},
      {"t":"h","text":"A road-test method"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check the tyres first: pressure, damage and wear. Many vibrations are tyre-related.","Drive at the speed the customer complains about, on a smooth road, and note when the problem begins and ends.","Try drive, coast and neutral at that speed. Press the clutch or select neutral at the vibration speed. If it stays, it is from the road wheels or driveline. If it goes, it is from the engine or gearbox.","Vary the speed slowly, in a higher or a lower gear, to see whether the frequency follows the road speed or the engine speed.","Turn gently left and right. Joint and bearing noises change with load.","Use a **stethoscope** or a vibration app to locate the source, and put the car on a lift with the wheels spinning to listen, with extreme care."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common causes by symptom"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Common causes"],"rows":[["Shake at 80 to 110 km/h that goes away at other speeds","Out-of-balance wheel or bent rim. Rebalance and check run-out."],["Vibration under acceleration only","Worn inner CV joint, bent driveshaft, or a collapsed mount"],["Humming that rises with speed and changes when turning","Wheel bearing"],["Clicking when turning on full lock","Outer CV joint"],["Clunk when taking up drive or releasing the throttle","Mount, U-joint, differential backlash or CV joint"],["Rumble at the same speed in all gears","Tyre, wheel or bearing"],["Whine that changes in drive and coast","Gears in the differential or gearbox, with wear or low oil"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Link the symptom to a speed. A part that rotates at that speed is the suspect, so measure the speeds and frequencies."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never run a vehicle with the drive wheels off the ground without secure stands and a safe procedure, and keep clear of rotating shafts, joints and wheels."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m16-l9", "title": "Gearbox and driveline faults", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Driveline faults are diagnosed by road test, inspection and fluid checks. This lesson gives a method."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Hard to shift"},
      {"t":"p","text":"If a gearbox is hard to shift, first decide if it is the clutch, the linkage or the gearbox itself. If it is hard to select gears when the vehicle is stopped with the pedal down, but easy when the engine is off, the clutch is not releasing. Air in the hydraulics, a worn master or slave cylinder or a stretched cable are the usual causes. If it is hard in every case, check the oil level and type, the linkage and the cables for damage and then the synchronisers."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Jumping out of gear"},
      {"t":"p","text":"If the gearbox pops out of a gear, the cause may be worn selector parts, a weak detent spring, worn synchroniser sleeves, wear on the gear dogs or a loose or broken engine or gearbox mount, which allows the gearbox to move. Check the mounts first, because they are cheapest."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Whine and oil leaks"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A whine usually means low oil or worn bearings or gears. Check the oil level and condition, and look for metal particles on the drain plug magnet. Oil leaks at the input shaft seal, output seals, drain plug and cover joints are common. A leak at the bell housing can mean an input shaft seal, which may be mistaken for an engine rear main seal leak. Oil on the clutch will make it slip or judder."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Clutch judder"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Judder means that the clutch grabs and releases in a jerky way. Causes include oil or grease on the disc, hot spots or cracks on the flywheel, a distorted disc or pressure plate, a worn dual-mass flywheel or loose or worn mounts."},
      {"t":"h","text":"A diagnostic approach"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Ask the customer when the fault happens: moving off, in particular gears, when turning, at speed.","Check the oil level and condition, and the clutch fluid.","Road test, noting the noise, the vibration and the gears involved.","Inspect the mounts, CV joints and shafts on the stands or a lift.","Check the clutch pedal for free play and travel, and test the hydraulics.","Decide the cause from the evidence before you remove the gearbox."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Where to look first","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Noise in neutral that stops when the clutch pedal is pressed","Input shaft bearing, dual-mass flywheel","A worn bearing or flywheel"],["Noise in one gear only","The gear pair for that gear","A worn or chipped gear, or a worn bearing"],["Noise in all gears, louder with speed","Main bearings or the final drive","Worn bearings or low oil"],["Clunk when taking up drive","Mounts, joints, differential backlash","Worn mounts, CV or U-joints, or excessive backlash"],["Grinding when changing gear","Clutch release, synchroniser","A dragging clutch or worn synchro rings"],["Oil leak at the bell housing","Input shaft seal, rear main seal","Seal failure"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Removing a gearbox to find a fault is costly. Rule out the simple causes first: fluid, mounts, linkage and the hydraulics."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"A drain plug magnet with a little fine grey paste is normal. Large chips or sharp metal pieces are not."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m16-l10", "title": "Service and repair", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Servicing the gearbox and replacing a clutch are common jobs. They need the right order and care, because the parts are heavy and tolerances are small."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Changing gearbox or axle oil"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Park the vehicle level on a lift or stands, and have a drain container ready.","First loosen the **fill plug**. If you can not open it, do not drain the oil, because you would not be able to refill.","Remove the drain plug and let the oil drain. Check the magnet.","Clean the plug, fit a new washer, and tighten it to the manual's torque.","Fill through the fill hole with the correct oil until it just begins to run out. Refit the fill plug with a new seal at the torque."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Replacing a clutch"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Disconnect the battery. Remove the gearbox, using a transmission jack and following the manual's order. Support the engine as the manual requires.","Mark the position of the pressure plate and flywheel, then loosen the pressure plate bolts evenly and in a cross pattern, to release the spring force evenly.","Inspect the flywheel for cracks, hot spots and wear. Resurface or replace it, or replace the dual-mass flywheel as needed.","Fit the new disc the right way round (the marking says 'flywheel side' or similar) and **centre it with an alignment tool**, so the gearbox input shaft will fit.","Fit the new pressure plate, and tighten the bolts evenly and in a cross pattern to the torque.","Replace the release bearing and the pilot bearing or bush. Lightly grease the input shaft splines and the release bearing contact points, as the manual says, without getting grease on the friction surfaces.","Refit the gearbox, making sure that the shaft enters the disc without being forced. Never pull the gearbox into place with the bolts.","Refill the oil if lost, bleed the hydraulic clutch if fitted, and test."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Bleeding a hydraulic clutch"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Fill the reservoir with the correct fluid, open the bleed screw on the slave cylinder with a hose into a container, and press the pedal slowly until clear fluid with no bubbles comes out. Close the screw before releasing the pedal, and keep the reservoir topped up. Some systems need a pressure bleeder."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Safety with heavy parts"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A gearbox can weigh 30 to 100 kg or more. Use a transmission jack with a safety strap, and keep your hands and body clear of the load. Support the engine whenever a mount or the gearbox is removed."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Clutch replacement: what to check and renew"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Clutch kit:** disc, pressure plate and release bearing, renewed together. A new disc on a worn pressure plate wears quickly.","**Flywheel:** check the friction face for heat spots, cracks, scoring and run-out. Resurface within the maker's limits or replace. A dual-mass flywheel is checked for play and for grease leaks.","**Input shaft:** check the splines for wear and the pilot bearing or bush in the flywheel, and replace if worn.","**Spigot and seal:** replace the crankshaft rear seal or the gearbox input seal if there is any leak.","**Hydraulics:** replace the slave cylinder (and often the master cylinder) if it leaks, or at clutch replacement if the maker advises.","**Release fork and pivot:** inspect the contact points and lubricate with the correct grease."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Never get grease or oil on the clutch lining or the flywheel face. Wear gloves and keep the new disc clean. Use the aligning tool to centre the disc before you tighten the pressure plate, then tighten the bolts evenly to the torque in the manual. Never use compressed air to remove clutch dust."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never use compressed air to blow clutch dust. Use a damp cloth or an approved dust-capture cleaner, and wear a mask and gloves."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A new clutch on a damaged flywheel or with oil leaking onto it will fail quickly. Fix the cause of the oil leak, such as the main seal or input shaft seal, while the gearbox is out."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m16-l11", "title": "Overhauling gearboxes and differentials: principles", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Most workshops replace gearboxes and axles with reconditioned units, but understanding how they are measured and rebuilt is essential to deciding what is worn, and to doing small repairs such as bearings, seals and synchronisers. This lesson explains the principles of end float, preload and backlash, the checks on gears, and how to rebuild safely."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Is the overhaul worth it?"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Before a gearbox is taken apart, think about the cost of the parts and labour, the availability of parts and special tools, and the cost of a reconditioned unit. A single worn synchroniser ring or bearing can often be replaced, but a unit with many worn parts, a damaged case or a failed gear set is often cheaper to replace. Give the customer the options in writing."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Measuring wear"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Measurement","What it shows","How"],"rows":[["End float of a shaft","The amount a shaft can move along its length. Too much means worn bearings or thrust washers.","Dial gauge on the end of the shaft, push and pull"],["Gear backlash","The small gap between meshing teeth. Too much gives noise and clunk. Too little causes a whine and heat.","Dial gauge on a gear tooth, or feeler gauge"],["Synchroniser ring clearance","The gap between the ring and the gear when pressed together. Too small means a worn ring.","Feeler gauge between the ring and the gear cone"],["Gear tooth wear","Pitting, chipping, scoring or polished patches","Visual inspection and comparison with the manual"],["Shaft run-out and bearing seats","A bent shaft or worn seats","V-blocks and a dial gauge, and a micrometer"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Bearing preload and shimming"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Tapered roller bearings work in pairs and need a small, controlled amount of **preload** (squeeze), set with shims, a spacer or a nut. Too little preload gives play, noise and wear. Too much overheats the bearing and burns it out. A differential and final drive also need the pinion depth and the crown wheel backlash to be set exactly, with shims, to get the correct tooth contact pattern."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Setting end float with a shim","steps":["The manual gives a shaft end float of 0.05 to 0.15 mm. The reading with the old shim is 0.32 mm, which is too much by about 0.22 mm (to reach a target of 0.10 mm).","A thicker shim reduces the end float. The old shim is 1.20 mm. A new shim of 1.20 + 0.22 = 1.42 mm gives about 0.10 mm.","Fit the new shim, tighten to torque, and measure again. Repeat if the reading is outside the range. Always measure after you fit."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading a crown wheel contact pattern"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Paint the teeth with marking compound, turn the pinion several times with a light load on the crown wheel and read the pattern. A good pattern is oval and centred, a little toward the toe on the drive side. A pattern near the heel or the root, or at the top, shows the pinion depth or backlash is wrong, and the shims must be changed. Only specialist workshops should set up a hypoid pair, as it needs the manual's pattern chart and the tools."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Rebuilding good practice"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Clean the gearbox outside, drain the oil and examine it. Metal in the oil shows the type and the cause of wear.","Take it apart in the order of the manual. Keep the shims, spacers and thrust washers in order and note their positions.","Clean all parts and look for wear and damage. Measure the parts.","Replace bearings, seals, circlips, gaskets and any damaged parts, always in sets. A worn gear and a new gear running against it wear quickly, so replace both gears of a pair.","Press bearings on using the right driver on the correct ring, and never hit a bearing.","Assemble with clean oil. Set the end floats and backlashes, and tighten the bolts to the specified torque.","Check that every gear can be selected and the shafts turn freely by hand before you refit the unit.","Fill with the correct oil to the correct level, and run in carefully."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Measure, do not guess. End float, preload and backlash are measured, and set with shims, and they decide how long the rebuild will last."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Gearbox parts are heavy and sharp. Use a gearbox jack and stands, and never trust a jack alone. Wear eye protection when pressing and when using circlip pliers."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Inspect a clutch and flywheel, check CV joints and change gearbox oil","objective":"Inspect and measure a clutch disc and flywheel, check CV joint boots and play on a vehicle, and change the gearbox oil using the correct procedure.","time":"120 minutes","tools":["A clutch assembly (disc, pressure plate, flywheel) on the bench","Vernier calliper or rivet depth gauge, straightedge and feeler gauges","Vehicle on a lift or axle stands, with the manual","Socket set, torque wrench, drain container and oil pump","Correct gearbox oil and a new drain plug washer"],"safety":["Support the vehicle on a lift or axle stands. Never work under a vehicle on a jack alone.","Do not blow dust with compressed air. Use a damp cloth or an approved cleaner.","Wear gloves and safety glasses, and wipe up spilled oil at once.","Open the fill plug before you remove the drain plug.","Dispose of the old oil correctly."],"steps":["Examine the clutch disc for wear, cracks, oil contamination and loose rivets.","Measure the thickness of the friction material or rivet depth, and compare with the limit.","Check the pressure plate for hot spots, cracks and the diaphragm spring condition.","Check the flywheel face for scoring, hot spots and runout, and measure its flatness with a straightedge.","Spin the release bearing by hand, and note any roughness or noise.","Raise the vehicle on a lift or stands and inspect each CV boot for splits and grease leaks.","Hold each shaft and check for play in the CV joints.","Try the fill plug first, and then place the drain container under the drain plug.","Drain the oil and check the drain plug magnet for debris.","Fit a new washer and tighten the drain plug to torque.","Fill with the correct oil until it reaches the fill hole, and tighten the fill plug to torque.","Record all measurements and write your findings."],"checks":["I measured the disc and flywheel and compared them with the limits.","I found and described any CV joint boot or joint faults.","I opened the fill plug before draining the oil.","I used the correct oil and torque and disposed of the old oil correctly.","I did not use compressed air on the clutch dust."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m16-q01","q":"In a rear-wheel-drive vehicle, what is the path of power after the gearbox?","options":["Differential, clutch, propeller shaft, axle shafts and wheels","Propeller shaft, differential, axle shafts and wheels","Flywheel, propeller shaft, gearbox, differential and wheels","Axle shafts, clutch, differential, propeller shaft and wheels"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m16-l1","explain":"Power goes from the gearbox along the propeller shaft to the differential, then to the wheels through the axles."},
    {"id":"m16-q02","q":"A driving gear with 20 teeth meshes with a driven gear with 60 teeth. What is the ratio?","options":["1 to 3","20 to 60 turns of output per input turn","3 to 1","6 to 1"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l1","explain":"Ratio = driven teeth ÷ driving teeth = 60 ÷ 20 = 3. The output turns more slowly with more torque."},
    {"id":"m16-q03","q":"What does a low gear, such as first, give?","options":["The same torque and speed as other gears","Less torque and a higher speed","More torque and a lower speed","No change in torque"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m16-l1","explain":"Low gears have high ratios, which multiply the torque."},
    {"id":"m16-q04","q":"The second gear is 2.0:1 and the final drive is 4.0:1. What is the overall ratio?","options":["2:1","6:1","0.5:1","8:1"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m16-l1","explain":"The overall ratio is the gearbox ratio multiplied by the final drive ratio: 2.0 × 4.0 = 8."},
    {"id":"m16-q05","q":"Which layout has the engine at the front driving the front wheels?","options":["Rear-wheel drive (RWD) layout","Front-wheel drive (FWD)","Mid-engine drive layout of cars","Rear-engine drive layout"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m16-l1","explain":"FWD cars use a transaxle with two driveshafts to the front wheels. RWD cars send the power to the rear through a propeller shaft."},
    {"id":"m16-q06","q":"A gearbox ratio is 1.32 and the final drive is 4.10. What is the overall ratio?","options":["2.79","3.10","5.41","0.32"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l2","explain":"Overall ratio = 1.32 × 4.10 = 5.41. Each ratio multiplies the next."},
    {"id":"m16-q07","q":"At 3,000 rpm in a gear with an overall ratio of 5.41, and a tyre circumference of 1.985 m, what is the road speed?","options":["About 111 km/h","About 30 km/h","About 220 km/h","About 66 km/h"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m16-l2","explain":"Wheel speed = 3,000 ÷ 5.41 = 554 rpm. Speed = 554 × 1.985 × 60 ÷ 1,000 = 66 km/h."},
    {"id":"m16-q08","q":"Engine torque is 200 N·m in a gear with an overall ratio of 14.68 and 92% efficiency. What is the torque at the wheels?","options":["About 2,700 N·m","About 200 N·m","About 14 N·m","About 27,000 N·m"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l2","explain":"Torque at the wheels = 200 × 14.68 × 0.92 = 2,701 N·m. Gearing multiplies torque."},
    {"id":"m16-q09","q":"Larger tyres are fitted. What happens to the speedometer reading?","options":["It reads higher than the true speed","It does not change","It reads lower than the true speed","It stops working"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l2","explain":"The speedometer counts rotations. A larger tyre covers more road per turn, so the car goes faster than the display shows."},
    {"id":"m16-q10","q":"What does the clutch disc connect to on the gearbox side?","options":["The layshaft, by bolts","The differential, by a belt","The output shaft, by a chain","The input shaft, by splines"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l3","explain":"The disc's hub slides on the splines of the gearbox input shaft."},
    {"id":"m16-q11","q":"What clamps the clutch disc against the flywheel?","options":["The release bearing, which sits on the front of the gearbox","The selector fork, which slides the gear sleeve along the shaft","The pressure plate with its diaphragm spring","The pilot bearing, which sits in the end of the crankshaft"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l3","explain":"The spring supplies the clamping force. The pedal releases it."},
    {"id":"m16-q12","q":"What does the release bearing do?","options":["It supports the end of the crankshaft inside the engine block","It pushes on the diaphragm spring to release the clamp when the pedal is pressed","It selects the gears inside the gearbox when the lever moves","It holds the disc firmly against the flywheel at all times"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m16-l3","explain":"It is also called the throw-out bearing."},
    {"id":"m16-q13","q":"The engine revs rise when you accelerate, but the vehicle speed does not. What is the problem?","options":["A slipping clutch","A worn CV joint on the front axle of the vehicle","A dragging clutch that will not release the disc fully","A flat tyre on one of the driven wheels of the car"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l3","explain":"Power is lost between the engine and the gearbox because the disc can not hold."},
    {"id":"m16-q14","q":"Hard gear selection with the engine running and the pedal down, but easy with the engine off. What does this suggest?","options":["The battery is weak and cannot run the starter motor","The clutch is not fully releasing, for example due to air in the hydraulics","The gearbox oil is too clean and is not lubricating the gears","The tyres are over-inflated and are giving a hard ride"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m16-l3","explain":"Check the fluid, the master and slave cylinders and the pedal free play."},
    {"id":"m16-q15","q":"What is a dual-mass flywheel designed to do?","options":["Store extra fuel for the engine when it is idling","Make the engine start more easily on a cold morning","Absorb engine vibrations with two parts joined by springs","Cool the clutch disc by passing air across its face"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l3","explain":"A worn one can rattle at idle and cause judder."},
    {"id":"m16-q16","q":"A burning smell after a hill start with a heavy load suggests what?","options":["The brakes are stone cold and dry","The engine oil is brand new now","The tyres are over-inflated a lot","The clutch overheated slipping"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l3","explain":"Slipping turns the engine's work into heat in the lining and the flywheel. Overheating glazes and wears the friction material."},
    {"id":"m16-q17","q":"A clutch has μ = 0.30, clamp force 6,000 N, mean radius 0.10 m and two friction faces. What is its torque capacity?","options":["360 N·m","180 N·m","1,800 N·m","36 N·m"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l4","explain":"Capacity = 0.30 × 6,000 × 0.10 × 2 = 360 N·m. It must exceed the engine's peak torque."},
    {"id":"m16-q18","q":"Oil gets on a clutch lining and μ halves. What happens to the capacity?","options":["It doubles, so the grip is better","It stays exactly the same as before","It drops to zero at once always","It halves and may slip"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m16-l4","explain":"Capacity is proportional to the friction coefficient. Half the friction gives half the capacity, which may be below the engine torque."},
    {"id":"m16-q19","q":"Air in a clutch hydraulic system causes what?","options":["A pedal that is much too hard to push","A soft pedal and poor release","A louder engine note at idle speed","A faster gear change than before"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l4","explain":"Air is compressed by the master cylinder, which takes up the pedal travel before the slave cylinder moves."},
    {"id":"m16-q20","q":"A pedal ratio of 4:1, with 100 N foot force and a master cylinder area of 283.5 mm², gives what pressure?","options":["About 14 bar","About 1.4 bar","About 140 bar","About 0.14 bar"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m16-l4","explain":"Force at the master = 100 × 4 = 400 N. Pressure = 400 ÷ 283.5 = 1.41 N/mm², which is 14.1 bar."},
    {"id":"m16-q21","q":"What does a rattle at idle that vanishes when the clutch pedal is pressed suggest?","options":["A worn brake pad on one wheel","A worn DMF or input bearing","A low coolant level in the radiator","A faulty wiper motor in the cowl"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l4","explain":"Pressing the pedal disconnects the engine from the gearbox. The noise source is therefore in the parts the clutch drives."},
    {"id":"m16-q22","q":"What is the purpose of a synchroniser?","options":["To lock the gearbox in neutral while the engine is idling","To match the speeds of the gear and shaft so the gear can be engaged without grinding","To pump oil around the gearbox when the vehicle is moving","To reverse the direction of the output shaft in reverse gear"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m16-l5","explain":"A cone clutch between the sleeve and the gear matches the speeds first."},
    {"id":"m16-q23","q":"What does the layshaft do?","options":["It connects the gearbox to the clutch pedal through a linkage","It carries a cluster of gears that always turn with the input shaft","It carries the gears that the driver selects with the shift lever","It drives the speedometer cable from the output shaft"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l5","explain":"In a constant-mesh gearbox, the input shaft always drives the layshaft."},
    {"id":"m16-q24","q":"What does a selector fork do?","options":["It holds the clutch disc in place against the flywheel face","It turns the layshaft while the engine is running in neutral","It slides the synchroniser sleeve to engage a gear","It cools the gearbox oil as it passes round the casing"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m16-l5","explain":"The fork is moved by the shift linkage when you move the lever, and it pushes the sleeve towards the gear that you want to engage."},
    {"id":"m16-q25","q":"A gearbox whines in one gear only. What is the likely cause?","options":["A flat battery that cannot power the gearbox electronics","A leak at the drain plug that has let some of the oil out","A worn clutch disc that is slipping under load in that gear","A worn gear pair or bearing for that gear"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m16-l5","explain":"The noise is tied to the gear that is carrying the load."},
    {"id":"m16-q26","q":"Why must the correct gearbox oil be used?","options":["Gearbox oil is only there for cooling, so the type is not important","The wrong oil can cause hard shifts or damage soft parts such as synchronisers","The colour of the oil must match the paint on the gearbox","Any oil will do so long as the level is correct for the box"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l5","explain":"Gearbox oils differ, and the wrong type can make shifting hard or attack soft parts, so always follow the manual's specification."},
    {"id":"m16-q27","q":"What is the correct oil level in a gearbox with a fill plug?","options":["Level with the bottom of the fill hole, with the vehicle level","Above the fill hole by about a centimetre, with the car level","Half way up the dipstick when the gearbox is cold and at rest","Filled until it flows out of the breather on the top of the casing"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l5","explain":"The level is set by the fill hole with the vehicle level; too little oil causes wear, and too much can leak past seals and foam."},
    {"id":"m16-q28","q":"How does a dual-clutch gearbox avoid a break in drive?","options":["It uses a torque converter","It uses a single clutch with a flywheel","It slips both clutches at once","One clutch releases as one engages"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m16-l6","explain":"The next gear is already selected on the other shaft. The change is made by swapping clutches."},
    {"id":"m16-q29","q":"What should be done after a clutch replacement on an AMT or DCT?","options":["Add more oil to the gearbox","Drive hard to bed the clutch in","Relearn the touch point","Nothing, as the system learns by itself"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l6","explain":"The computer must learn where the new clutch begins to grip. Without it, gear changes are rough and the clutch wears quickly."},
    {"id":"m16-q30","q":"Which advice helps to protect a dry DCT clutch in traffic?","options":["Hold the car on a hill with the throttle","Use the brake to hold on a slope","Creep forward in neutral","Rev the engine at each stop"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m16-l6","explain":"Holding with the throttle makes the clutch slip continuously, which creates heat and wear."},
    {"id":"m16-q31","q":"A DCT shudders pulling away. What should you check early?","options":["The windscreen washer fluid level","The air filter in the airbox","The interior lights and switches","Software, fluid and adaptations"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l6","explain":"Many DCT judder faults are corrected by an update or relearn. Fluid and clutch condition matter in a wet DCT."},
    {"id":"m16-q32","q":"What does a clicking noise on a full-lock turn usually indicate?","options":["A worn clutch","A worn outer CV joint","A worn gearbox bearing","A weak battery"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m16-l7","explain":"It often follows a split boot that let the grease out."},
    {"id":"m16-q33","q":"A front-wheel-drive car vibrates when accelerating hard. Which part is a likely suspect?","options":["A worn inner CV joint","A worn outer CV joint","A worn fuel pump","A worn clutch release bearing"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l7","explain":"Inner joints cause vibration under load. Outer joints click on turns."},
    {"id":"m16-q34","q":"What does a split CV boot lead to?","options":["Grease escapes and dirt gets in, which ruins the joint","The tyre wears unevenly on the inside edge of the tread","The gearbox oil drains out through the driveshaft seal at once","The brake fluid leaks from the caliper on the same wheel"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m16-l7","explain":"Replace the boot early or replace the joint if it has run dry."},
    {"id":"m16-q35","q":"What does a humming or growling noise that gets louder with speed suggest?","options":["A worn spark plug that is misfiring at higher engine speeds","A worn clutch plate that is slipping under heavy load","A worn wheel bearing","A worn wiper blade that is squeaking on the glass of the screen"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l7","explain":"Worn wheel bearings hum or growl more as speed rises, and rocking the wheel by hand with the vehicle lifted can reveal play."},
    {"id":"m16-q36","q":"What is the purpose of a centre bearing on a propeller shaft?","options":["To support a long shaft made in two parts","To lock the differential when the vehicle is on loose ground","To cool the shaft by passing oil over it as it turns","To drive the speedometer from the output shaft of the gearbox"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m16-l7","explain":"A worn bearing or its rubber can cause vibration."},
    {"id":"m16-q37","q":"A single universal joint works at 10°. By roughly how much does its output speed vary?","options":["About ±1.5%","About ±15%","About ±0.15%","About ±50%"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m16-l7","explain":"The output varies between cos 10° (0.985) and 1 ÷ cos 10° (1.015) times the input speed."},
    {"id":"m16-q38","q":"A vehicle travels at 100 km/h with a wheel speed of 14 Hz. A vibration is felt at 14 Hz. What is likely?","options":["The engine or its accessories","The clutch pedal linkage","A wheel or tyre problem","The air filter and its housing"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l8","explain":"The first-order vibration of a wheel occurs at wheel speed. A shaft or engine would show a different frequency."},
    {"id":"m16-q39","q":"The final drive ratio is 4.1 and the wheel speed is 14 Hz. At what frequency does the propeller shaft turn?","options":["About 57 Hz","About 14 Hz","About 3.4 Hz","About 140 Hz"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m16-l8","explain":"Propeller shaft speed = wheel speed × final drive = 14 × 4.1 = 57.4 Hz."},
    {"id":"m16-q40","q":"Vibration stays the same when the clutch is pressed at speed. What does this mean?","options":["It comes from the engine","It comes from the clutch","It comes from the air filter","It comes from the road wheels or the driveline"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l8","explain":"Pressing the clutch removes the engine. A vibration that persists comes from the parts that rotate with the wheels."},
    {"id":"m16-q41","q":"Which fault usually clicks when turning on full lock?","options":["A worn inner CV joint","A flat battery","A worn outer CV joint","A blown fuse"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m16-l8","explain":"The outer CV joint works at the greatest angle when steering. Wear and a dry joint produce a click."},
    {"id":"m16-q42","q":"A gearbox jumps out of third gear. What should be checked first, as the cheapest and quickest?","options":["The propeller shaft, because it carries the drive to the axle","The gearbox internal parts, since they are the most likely cause","The flywheel, because it holds the clutch in place at the rear","The engine and gearbox mounts"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l9","explain":"A loose mount lets the gearbox move and pull the gear out."},
    {"id":"m16-q43","q":"What can cause clutch judder when moving off?","options":["A weak horn that is drawing current from the battery","Too much gearbox oil in the casing that is foaming at speed","Oil on the disc, hot spots on the flywheel or worn mounts","A leaking brake hose that is dripping fluid onto the clutch"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m16-l9","explain":"Oil or grease on the disc, hot spots on the flywheel or loose mounts make the clutch grab unevenly, so fix the cause of any oil leak as well."},
    {"id":"m16-q44","q":"A whining gearbox has a drain plug magnet covered in large metal chips. What does this tell you?","options":["It is normal wear, so no action is needed","The gearbox has internal damage and needs further inspection","The oil is too thin and should be topped up with water","The clutch disc is contaminated with grease"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m16-l9","explain":"A little fine grey paste is normal. Large chips or sharp pieces mean parts are breaking up inside."},
    {"id":"m16-q45","q":"It is hard to select gears with the engine running and the clutch pedal down, but easy with the engine off. What is the likely cause?","options":["The clutch is not releasing fully","The gearbox oil level is too high","The propeller shaft is out of balance","The tyres are under-inflated"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l9","explain":"If the gears go in easily with the engine off, the gearbox is fine and the clutch is still dragging. Check for air in the hydraulics, a worn cylinder or a stretched cable."},
    {"id":"m16-q46","q":"A noise in neutral that stops when the clutch pedal is pressed suggests what?","options":["A worn brake disc on a wheel","A bad wheel bearing at the rear","An input bearing or DMF fault","A blocked exhaust silencer box"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l9","explain":"The noise source is on the engine side of the clutch, or in the input shaft, which rotates when the clutch is engaged."},
    {"id":"m16-q47","q":"What should you do before removing the drain plug to change gearbox oil?","options":["Remove the clutch first so that the oil can drain freely","Run the engine at high speed to warm the oil before you start","Disconnect the battery so that the oil can be drained safely","Open the fill plug first to be sure that you can refill the gearbox"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m16-l10","explain":"If the fill plug is seized, you can not refill it."},
    {"id":"m16-q48","q":"How should the pressure plate bolts be loosened and tightened?","options":["Evenly, in a cross pattern","In a clockwise circle with an impact gun","One at a time, fully, from one side","Only the top two bolts"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l10","explain":"Even tightening avoids distorting the cover."},
    {"id":"m16-q49","q":"Why is an alignment tool used when fitting a clutch disc?","options":["To centre the disc so that the gearbox input shaft will enter it","To tighten the pressure plate bolts evenly in one pass","To clean the face of the flywheel before it is refitted","To bleed the air from the hydraulic clutch system afterwards"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l10","explain":"A misaligned disc will make the gearbox impossible to refit."},
    {"id":"m16-q50","q":"What must you never do when refitting a gearbox?","options":["Check that the dowels line up with the holes in the casing","Align the input shaft with the splines of the clutch disc","Support it with a transmission jack that has a safety strap","Pull the gearbox into position using the bolts"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m16-l10","explain":"Forcing it can damage the disc, shaft and bellhousing."},
    {"id":"m16-q51","q":"What should you do with oil leaking onto a clutch?","options":["Fit the new clutch and ignore the leak, since it is only a small one","Fix the leak, such as a main seal or input shaft seal, while the gearbox is out","Add more gearbox oil so that the leak does not matter in use","Wipe the disc with an oily rag so that it grips more evenly"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l10","explain":"Oil on a new clutch makes it slip or judder."},
    {"id":"m16-q52","q":"How is a hydraulic clutch bled?","options":["Press the pedal slowly with the bleed screw open until clear fluid with no bubbles flows","Press the pedal fast with the bleed screw tightly closed","Drain the whole system and leave it empty until the next day","Fill the system with engine oil instead of clutch fluid"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m16-l10","explain":"Keep the reservoir topped up and close the screw before releasing the pedal."},
    {"id":"m16-q53","q":"Why should compressed air not be used to blow clutch dust?","options":["The air would remove the grease from the splines of the shaft","The air would cool the disc too fast and the friction material would crack","The dust is too heavy to blow away from the housing with air","The dust may contain harmful fibres that could be breathed in"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l10","explain":"Use a damp cloth or an approved cleaner."},
    {"id":"m16-q54","q":"Which parts are normally replaced together in a clutch job?","options":["The disc only, and nothing else","The gearbox oil filter and pan","Disc, plate and release bearing","The spark plugs of every cylinder"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l10","explain":"A new disc running on a worn pressure plate wears fast. The release bearing is inexpensive and difficult to reach again."},
    {"id":"m16-q55","q":"Why must grease be kept off the clutch lining and the flywheel face?","options":["Grease makes the lining thicker","Grease increases the torque capacity","Grease makes the clutch quieter","It makes the clutch slip or judder"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m16-l10","explain":"Oil and grease lower friction. A contaminated clutch slips or chatters."},
    {"id":"m16-q56","q":"A shaft has end float of 0.32 mm and the target is 0.10 mm. The shim is 1.20 mm. What shim is needed?","options":["About 0.98 mm","About 1.42 mm","About 1.20 mm","About 0.22 mm"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l11","explain":"A thicker shim reduces end float. Add the excess: 1.20 + 0.22 = 1.42 mm."},
    {"id":"m16-q57","q":"Why are both gears of a worn pair replaced together?","options":["A new gear on a worn one wears fast","Gears are only sold in pairs by law","New gears are always lighter","It makes the oil change colour"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m16-l11","explain":"Mating teeth wear to match. A new gear against a worn one has a poor contact and fails soon."},
    {"id":"m16-q58","q":"What does too little bearing preload cause?","options":["Overheating and burning out","Play, noise and wear","A blown fuse","A softer gear change"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m16-l11","explain":"Without enough preload, the bearing is loose. Too much preload causes heat and a burnt bearing."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m16-c01","front":"What is the torque path in a rear-wheel-drive car?","back":"Engine, clutch, gearbox, propeller shaft, differential, axles, wheels.","lesson":"m16-l1"},
    {"id":"m16-c02","front":"How do you calculate a gear ratio?","back":"Driven teeth divided by driving teeth.","lesson":"m16-l1"},
    {"id":"m16-c03","front":"What does a low gear give?","back":"More torque and lower speed.","lesson":"m16-l1"},
    {"id":"m16-c04","front":"How do you find overall ratio?","back":"Gearbox ratio multiplied by the final drive ratio.","lesson":"m16-l1"},
    {"id":"m16-c05","front":"How is overall gear ratio worked out?","back":"Gearbox ratio times final drive ratio.","lesson":"m16-l2"},
    {"id":"m16-c06","front":"Road speed formula?","back":"rpm × circumference × 60 ÷ (overall ratio × 1,000), in km/h with circumference in metres.","lesson":"m16-l2"},
    {"id":"m16-c07","front":"Effect of larger tyres on the speedometer?","back":"It reads low, as each turn covers more road.","lesson":"m16-l2"},
    {"id":"m16-c08","front":"Torque at the wheels?","back":"Engine torque × overall ratio × efficiency.","lesson":"m16-l2"},
    {"id":"m16-c09","front":"What are the main clutch parts?","back":"Flywheel, disc, pressure plate, release bearing and pilot bearing.","lesson":"m16-l3"},
    {"id":"m16-c10","front":"What does the pressure plate do?","back":"It clamps the disc against the flywheel with a diaphragm spring.","lesson":"m16-l3"},
    {"id":"m16-c11","front":"What does the release bearing do?","back":"It pushes the diaphragm spring to release the clamp.","lesson":"m16-l3"},
    {"id":"m16-c12","front":"What does a slipping clutch feel like?","back":"Engine revs rise but the vehicle does not speed up.","lesson":"m16-l3"},
    {"id":"m16-c13","front":"What does a dragging clutch feel like?","back":"Gears are hard to select and may grind when stopped.","lesson":"m16-l3"},
    {"id":"m16-c14","front":"What is a dual-mass flywheel?","back":"A flywheel with two parts joined by springs that absorbs vibration.","lesson":"m16-l3"},
    {"id":"m16-c15","front":"Clutch torque capacity formula?","back":"μ × clamp force × mean radius × number of friction faces. Must exceed engine torque.","lesson":"m16-l4"},
    {"id":"m16-c16","front":"Typical friction coefficient of a lining?","back":"About 0.25 to 0.4. Oil can halve it.","lesson":"m16-l4"},
    {"id":"m16-c17","front":"Symptoms of a worn dual-mass flywheel?","back":"Rattle at idle that stops when the pedal is pressed, vibration at low speed and shudder when pulling away.","lesson":"m16-l4"},
    {"id":"m16-c18","front":"Why does air in clutch hydraulics matter?","back":"It is compressible and takes up pedal travel, so the clutch does not release.","lesson":"m16-l4"},
    {"id":"m16-c19","front":"What does a synchroniser do?","back":"It matches gear and shaft speeds so gears engage without grinding.","lesson":"m16-l5"},
    {"id":"m16-c20","front":"What does the layshaft do?","back":"It carries gears that always turn with the input shaft.","lesson":"m16-l5"},
    {"id":"m16-c21","front":"What does a selector fork do?","back":"It slides the synchroniser sleeve to engage a gear.","lesson":"m16-l5"},
    {"id":"m16-c22","front":"How is the gearbox oil level set?","back":"Level with the bottom of the fill hole, with the car level.","lesson":"m16-l5"},
    {"id":"m16-c23","front":"How does a DCT shift without a gap?","back":"Two clutches: odd and even gears. The next gear is pre-selected and the clutches swap.","lesson":"m16-l6"},
    {"id":"m16-c24","front":"Dry and wet DCT?","back":"Dry has dry clutches, efficient but heat-sensitive. Wet runs in oil, handles more torque and needs a regular oil change.","lesson":"m16-l6"},
    {"id":"m16-c25","front":"What must be done after replacing a DCT clutch?","back":"Relearn the adaptations and touch points with a scan tool.","lesson":"m16-l6"},
    {"id":"m16-c26","front":"What does clicking on turns mean?","back":"A worn outer CV joint.","lesson":"m16-l7"},
    {"id":"m16-c27","front":"What does vibration under acceleration mean?","back":"A worn inner CV joint or damaged shaft.","lesson":"m16-l7"},
    {"id":"m16-c28","front":"What does a split CV boot cause?","back":"Grease loss and dirt entry, which ruins the joint.","lesson":"m16-l7"},
    {"id":"m16-c29","front":"What does a humming noise rising with speed mean?","back":"A worn wheel bearing.","lesson":"m16-l7"},
    {"id":"m16-c30","front":"Why must a propeller shaft be refitted in phase?","back":"So the two universal joints cancel each other's speed variation.","lesson":"m16-l7"},
    {"id":"m16-c31","front":"What is first-order vibration?","back":"A vibration at the rotation frequency of a part, one cycle per turn.","lesson":"m16-l8"},
    {"id":"m16-c32","front":"Propeller shaft speed?","back":"Wheel speed times the final drive ratio.","lesson":"m16-l8"},
    {"id":"m16-c33","front":"How do you tell engine from driveline vibration?","back":"Press the clutch or select neutral at the speed. If it stops, it is the engine or gearbox.","lesson":"m16-l8"},
    {"id":"m16-c34","front":"What causes a gearbox to jump out of gear?","back":"Worn selector parts, weak detents, worn synchronisers or loose mounts.","lesson":"m16-l9"},
    {"id":"m16-c35","front":"What causes clutch judder?","back":"Oil on the disc, flywheel hot spots, distorted parts or worn mounts.","lesson":"m16-l9"},
    {"id":"m16-c36","front":"What is checked first with hard shifting?","back":"Clutch release, fluid, linkage and oil level and type.","lesson":"m16-l9"},
    {"id":"m16-c37","front":"What is done before draining gearbox oil?","back":"Open the fill plug first.","lesson":"m16-l10"},
    {"id":"m16-c38","front":"How are pressure plate bolts tightened?","back":"Evenly, in a cross pattern, to torque.","lesson":"m16-l10"},
    {"id":"m16-c39","front":"Why use an alignment tool?","back":"To centre the disc so the input shaft will enter.","lesson":"m16-l10"},
    {"id":"m16-c40","front":"How is a hydraulic clutch bled?","back":"Press slowly with the screw open until clear fluid without bubbles flows.","lesson":"m16-l10"},
    {"id":"m16-c41","front":"Why not blow clutch dust with air?","back":"It may contain harmful fibres.","lesson":"m16-l10"},
    {"id":"m16-c42","front":"Why shim tapered bearings?","back":"To set preload. Too little gives play and wear. Too much overheats the bearing.","lesson":"m16-l11"},
    {"id":"m16-c43","front":"What is backlash?","back":"The small gap between meshing gear teeth. Measured with a dial gauge.","lesson":"m16-l11"},
    {"id":"m16-c44","front":"New shim for end float too big?","back":"Add the excess to the old shim thickness.","lesson":"m16-l11"}
  ]
});
